00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.440
It's called time.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.660
And time is the kind of
thing that is like, oh,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.897
it's easy until you
start doing time zones.

00:02:23.897 --> 00:02:25.980
And when you think time
zones are hard, especially

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.396
in Australia-- I
don't know what's

00:02:27.396 --> 00:02:29.160
going on with that country.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.070
But then you need to start
thinking about things

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:37.020
like leap seconds and
stuff like that, which

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.950
we will have one this year.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.305
So we actually opened
an API to do this.

00:02:43.305 --> 00:02:44.055
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:44.055 --> 00:02:48.610
So we now have across Google, we
have these public NTP servers.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.990
So if you haven't ever used--
well you probably have used NTP

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.280
but you don't realize it.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.210
It's global services that are
provided to synchronize time

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.170
across machines.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.870
And we now have these
public NTP servers

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:05.160
that are available for you to
use that do the leap smearing

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.650
for leap seconds, so
that your machines don't

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.350
do weird things when they
think 61 seconds is good time

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.474
to have in a minute.

00:03:11.474 --> 00:03:12.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:12.889
Yeah.

00:03:12.889 --> 00:03:14.040
It is kind of cool.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.480
There's a gif that
I really like,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.380
which is a clock going--
it's 23, 59, and 58, all

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.910
the January-- no, December 31.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.910
And it goes 57, 58, 59, 60.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.390
So it counts 61 seconds in
the minute, which is actually

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.170
what will happen this year.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:33.150
So yeah.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.390
2016: longer than expected.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.930
And it's a really cool thing,
because not only you get access

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.800
to time that is very precise.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.425
You're actually
using load balancers.

00:03:43.425 --> 00:03:48.210
So you get access to the closest
atomic clock in the data center

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.620
where your instance is running.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.799
So it's incredibly accurate.

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:52.090
MARK MANDEL: That is very cool.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.548
I didn't realize
that was the case.

00:03:53.548 --> 00:03:54.450
That's pretty cool.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah
Probably way more accurate

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.110
that you will ever think.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:58.920
So yeah.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.090
It is incredibly powerful.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.090
It's a very, very
powerful clock.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:03.782
Yes.

00:04:03.782 --> 00:04:05.490
