00:07:01.611 --> 00:07:02.360
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.620
Cool.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is
a not very traditional way

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:09.650
to end up on Managed
Infrastructure.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.330
Why don't you tell
us a little bit

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.007
about what do you mean by
Managed Infrastructure?

00:07:14.007 --> 00:07:14.840
JERZY FORYCIARZ: OK.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:15.890
Let me go first.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:21.760
So Managed Infrastructure is a
suite of products and features

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.560
that are making the life
of a database easier.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.360
So we tend to call it as
viewer DevOps notion, right?

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.912
Something where you
really don't need

00:07:30.912 --> 00:07:32.370
to worry about your
infrastructure.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.360
You can focus just
on your applications.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.330
So in terms of what
we talk about in here,

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:41.420
we look at two
components, one of which

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.300
is the Managed Instance Groups.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.050
And I'll explain in a
second what this is.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:47.600
And the second one
is rightsizing.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.030
So a Managed Instance
Group is a way

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:54.090
of creating virtual
machines in a repeatable way

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:55.580
on a massive scale.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.640
So imagine you're
running a service.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:02.420
This service, let's
say it's a web service,

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:06.320
and it has front end running
on a virtual machine.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:12.820
But as the load is growing, you
need more and more connections

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:13.370
handled.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:15.330
And at some point,
there is not enough

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.010
that there is a single machine.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.550
So this system can be built
based on the Managed Instance

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:20.150
Group.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.830
And all of the logistics related
to adding instances, removing

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.570
instances automatically
is served by auto scaler.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.895
The fact that they're
kept alive and they

00:08:31.895 --> 00:08:35.330
are recreated whenever
there's any problem

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.070
is done by auto healer.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.080
And then you can
deploy a new version

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.650
of the software using app data
while your services are still

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:43.309
running.

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:45.200
So all of that, the
suite of products

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.260
we call Managed Infrastructure.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.320
And now the second piece
of it is the rightsizing.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:55.480
It's a way for us to tell you,
look, you're our great customer

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.830
and we don't want
you to overpay.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:08:59.000
It's one way of saying it.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.810
And second is,
look, we really see

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:08.600
that the setup on your machine
has too little of the memory

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:12.150
or not enough cords
to perform your task.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:15.320
So we get you rightsizing
recommendations and a way

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.220
to quickly actuate it so
that you are in a good shape.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:20.770
That's pretty much it.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:21.520
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:22.020
OK.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.390
So it sounds like there's--
basically it sounds like this

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:30.910
is a way to manage virtual
machines in a very elastic way

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:33.790
that enables you to scale.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:34.420
Excellent.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:34.919
Cool.

