00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.910
of what I would call canonical
typical use cases that we see.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.160
And there are some
that spin out of that.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.580
But let me start with the
simple, typical uses cases.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.971
And the first one is
anything that serves traffic.

00:10:09.971 --> 00:10:10.470
Right?

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.710
So typically that
could be, let's say,

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.710
a web front end
that either connects

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:19.120
to a phone application that
is distributed to your users

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.820
or maybe it's just
a classic store.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.530
And what makes it
really a good match

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:27.730
for Managed Instance Groups is
that first of all, your usage

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:28.501
is very dynamic.

00:10:28.501 --> 00:10:29.000
Right?

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:33.310
So as your traffic grows with,
let's say, the local time,

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:35.440
you need suddenly a
lot more processing.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.550
As much as twice or 10
times as much as you

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.070
need in your down times.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:42.270
And so you really want to take
advantage of this auto scaling,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:42.770
right?

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:46.510
Where as the traffic is
showing up in real time,

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.240
we'll be creating machines
in real time and scaling

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:52.450
up very, very quickly so
that you can take your peaks

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:54.560
and serve all of your customers.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.035
And then as soon as
the traffic stops,

00:10:57.035 --> 00:10:58.660
we'll get rid of
those machines, right?

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:02.140
So you don't have to be paying
for resources you don't use.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:05.590
And typically those
services are stateless.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:09.370
So each machine can basically be
brought up and then destroyed,

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:12.560
and it doesn't have
any local state.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:14.530
So it is very flexible that way.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:19.450
And the other use case that is
a very good usage of MIG's is

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:21.730
anything that can
or has to process,

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:24.730
let's say, lots of data
in a distributed fashion.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.890
So maybe you're
analyzing financial data.

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:30.370
So you have 10,000 stock
quotes and each one

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.110
can be analyzed independently.

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.680
So that can be distributed
over lots of machines.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.710
And then you want to do that as
you crunch through your data.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.420
But the other part
of that workload

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:42.730
is that it's very
much on demand, right?

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.670
So new financial data
might show up or maybe you

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.560
have a new model that
you want to analyze.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:50.620
And so you go from I need
none of this computed to I

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:52.510
need all of the
compute immediately,

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:55.060
as soon as you can give it
to me, to process through all

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:56.620
of this data in parallel.

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.494
So the MIG can scale
up very quickly,

00:11:58.494 --> 00:12:00.160
and basically give
you all the machines.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.100
You crunch through your
data and then it disappears.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:06.100
Another use case that's
typical is things that

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:07.810
work around image processing.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:12.700
So maybe you're getting a data
download of satellite imagery

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:15.490
or some other imagery that
comes maybe once a week.

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.880
And so again, you don't
need these machines six days

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:18.380
of the week.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:20.350
But one day of the week
you need all of them

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:24.044
because you've got terabytes
of data to crunch through.

00:12:24.044 --> 00:12:25.960
But each image can be
processed independently.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.280
So that's a perfect use case.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.790
So this is kind of like
distributed large scale data

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:31.630
processing.

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:34.210
And then another aspect that's
kind of orthogonal to this

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:38.620
but also important is this idea
of highly available services.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.290
So whether that's your
web front end or even

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.630
your data processor,
you want to make sure

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:47.770
that the machines will
recover if they crash or hang

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:49.270
or have some other issues.

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:52.630
And you don't have to yourself
go in there and fix it.

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.940
So with auto healing you
can monitor these machines.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:58.270
And if they run into trouble
your custom health check

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.550
will report on that and
the Managed Instance Group

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:03.107
will take care of this
and restart the machine.

00:13:03.107 --> 00:13:05.440
And then the other side of
the highly available equation

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:09.960
is this concept that, if all
the machines are in one zone--

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:13.660
our zones are stable but at the
same time outages can happen.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.150
And it is a best
engineering practice to say,

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:18.642
if you need a service to
be really, really stable,

00:13:18.642 --> 00:13:21.100
you don't want to put all of
your machines in a single zone

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:22.420
because things can happen.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.874
And so you want to spread
it across multiple zones.

00:13:24.874 --> 00:13:26.540
And so now with a
Managed Instance Group

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.470
you can essentially
spread your machines

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.490
evenly across three zones.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:34.710
So now we have something that's
called a regional Managed

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:35.420
Instance Group.

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:38.020
And we take care of this
problem of balancing.

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:40.750
So we'll add machines
and remove them as needed

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:44.440
so that each zone is
even to the other zones.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:46.074
So if any one of
them disappears,

00:13:46.074 --> 00:13:47.740
you can route your
traffic automatically

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:50.690
to the other zones and
not have any downtime.

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:51.349
So yeah.

00:13:51.349 --> 00:13:53.140
It's anything that's
basically distributed,

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:56.790
independent, stateless, and then
highly available requirements

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:58.682
is the additional piece.

00:13:58.682 --> 00:13:59.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:05.110
So nowadays, most people when
they start thinking about,

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:09.820
I have specific needs, like
you were mentioning-- video,

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:13.750
processing, or big
data processing that

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:15.580
needs to be done quite fast.

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:20.240
Most people would think about
using things like Kubernetes.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:23.380
What are the differences,
what are the benefits

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:27.020
of using Managed Instance Groups
or Managed Infrastructure,

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:30.040
as we call it, over going
with something like Kubernetes

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.255
or maybe even App Engine Flex?

00:14:32.255 --> 00:14:33.380
JERZY FORYCIARZ: All right.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:34.640
So let me take this one.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:38.270
So all of these products,
or we call them engines,

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:39.810
are complimentary.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.970
So you first need to
start from asking yourself

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:47.660
the question, how
much control do

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:52.730
I want to get versus how much
control do I want to give out?

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:57.050
And then we'll treat
everything automatic, right?

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.770
And from that perspective,
you have three choices, right?

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:03.830
You have App Engine or App
Engine Flex as one product.

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:08.180
It's best for the web
services of any kind.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:11.160
It's the easiest to start.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:14.630
There is some limitation
to the languages

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:20.990
that you can possibly use, but
you can load your custom Docker

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:24.080
containers and [INAUDIBLE]
it's the easiest

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:27.300
way for you to store it
on these kind of services.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:31.010
Now when you want
some more control,

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:36.620
or when you want to control
the cost of performance

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:38.990
or when you want super
fast auto scaler,

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:41.000
right, this already
factors where

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:45.154
that would tip you towards
the Google Compute Engine.

00:15:45.154 --> 00:15:47.070
So this is where you
have the Managed Instance

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:48.660
Group as a base service.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:49.160
Right?

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:53.030
So Google Compute Englines
are just your VM machines.

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:55.550
So Google Compute
Engine specifically

00:15:55.550 --> 00:16:00.860
is the platform on which App
Engine Flex is operating.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:05.130
And it's also a platform on
which Kubernetes is operating.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:05.720
Right?

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:07.580
So now, what
Kubernetes is adding

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:11.560
on top of Google Compute Engine
or a Managed Instance Group

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:12.930
is two factors, right?

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.900
It's the overall
container's orchestration

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.800
that contains of two
distinguishing factors, one

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.550
of which is bin packing
and the second one

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:23.660
is a general scheduling
slash microservices.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:27.830
So these two cannot be done on
any of these other platforms.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:31.400
Well they come as a service,
and what you have beneath

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:32.750
is a cluster of machines.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:37.118
And these machines are likely
run by the Managed Instance

00:16:37.118 --> 00:16:37.820
Groups.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:39.160
MARK MANDEL: So I guess if
we are thinking about it

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.120
in terms of the compute
continuum, right?

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:42.990
And on far, we'll say, left.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:43.740
Pick a direction.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:45.920
That's like, platform
is a service App Engine.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:49.830
Far right is GCE, like
straight-up infrastructure.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:51.290
This sits, I feel
like, somewhere

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:54.322
between Kubernetes and GCE.

00:16:54.322 --> 00:16:56.030
Like it sits in between
those two things,

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.970
where it's like it's not
entirely infrastructure

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:04.040
as a service and it's not nearly
as managed as, say, Kubernetes.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:07.250
But sort of sits in
between and gives you

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.950
a bit more flexibility
than, say, Kubernetes.

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:12.290
But a little bit more that
you need to manage yourself.

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:14.819
Would that be pretty accurate?

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:17.030
JERZY FORYCIARZ: I
think it's very close.

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:21.880
I think you have to remember
that Managed Instance Group is

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:22.800
GCE.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:24.319
This is exactly where we see it.

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:27.359
It's just a layer of
management on top of GCE.

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:27.859
But yes.

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:29.840
In terms of the
flexibility, this

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:31.280
is exactly where
you position it.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:36.890
Like GCE is one more level
higher in terms of abstraction.

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:41.060
You give up some of
the control and you

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:47.540
gain a lot of management in
terms of backing and scheduling

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.230
the containers on top
of a cluster of machines

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:52.540
that you don't necessarily
need to manage yourself.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:53.456
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:53.456 --> 00:17:56.540
So let's imagine
that I decide to--

00:17:56.540 --> 00:17:58.900
and actually I've
done it before.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:03.890
I need a bunch of machines
running Image Magic, right?

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:07.460
And what I'm going to do
is, I create a new instance

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:08.570
and I connect to it.

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:11.900
I do APT CAD, install,
install Image Magic,

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.750
and now I install my
software on top of it.

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:17.170
And everything is
ready and it works.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:20.240
Now normally if it
was in Kubernetes,

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.520
I could create a
docker image for that

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:24.680
and share that around, right?

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:28.460
How does that look on
Managed Instance Groups?

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:31.160
How do I make sure that
the new instances that

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:34.400
come into my Managed
Instance Group

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:38.047
have all the software and all
the features that I require?

00:18:38.047 --> 00:18:40.380
JERZY FORYCIARZ: Actually,
this is a very good question.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:44.860
So similarly to what you
can do on both App Engine

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:48.710
and Kubernetes, you can
get all of your software

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:51.350
into a docker container
and then deploy it

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:54.870
into the Managed Instance
Group in a similar way.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:59.270
The only difference is just
sitting as one container

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:00.590
on top of your VM.

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:06.930
So in that place comes the
need for deciding, very well,

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:11.450
how small your VM machine can be
so that your cost is optimized.

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:12.920
Everything else works the same.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.710
And really creating a
Managed Instance Group

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:18.770
is strictly defining
what type of a machine

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:20.210
I want to run, right?

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:23.430
And then where do I pull
my docker container from.

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.700
And so once we have these
two parameters defined--

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:29.570
we define them in something I
would call Instance Template--

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:30.920
then you can create a group.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:33.690
And once we have a group,
you can either tell it,

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:36.040
I want 100 instances,
1,000 instances,

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:37.980
2,000 instances of the same.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:40.260
Or you can enable
auto scale and say,

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:46.440
I want the average CPU
utilization to be around 60%.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:50.290
Or you can enable
auto healer and say,

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:54.870
if I don't hear from
this health check three

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:59.940
times in a row over a 30 second
period, consider it unhealthy

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:01.810
and please recreate
that instance for me

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:03.102
so that it continues operating.

00:20:03.102 --> 00:20:04.018
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:04.018 --> 00:20:05.871
So the question I'm
going to ask now--

00:20:05.871 --> 00:20:07.370
it's going to make
me look very bad.

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.270
But I actually
didn't know you could

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:12.900
run docker containers directly
onto an instance group.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:17.220
Do you need to choose a
specific image, a specific OS?

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:18.477
Or how does that work?

00:20:18.477 --> 00:20:20.310
JERZY FORYCIARZ: This
is a very new feature.

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:21.518
It's actually in [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:21.518 --> 00:20:24.990
So first of all, you have to
check on our documentation

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.630
or Google our
documentation and sign up.

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:32.910
And once you do, we'll enable
you, we will white list you.

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:35.670
And then the type of
image that runs there

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.400
is Google container image.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.507
And it automatically
runs a docker contain.

00:20:40.507 --> 00:20:41.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.254
So I feel less bad then.

00:20:43.254 --> 00:20:43.920
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:47.130
So for those who aren't
necessarily part of the Alpha

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:50.730
program or have, probably,
an investment in,

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.550
say-- I will say traditional
DevOps type tools, so Chef,

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.380
Puppet, Ansible, TerraForm.

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:58.440
If people want to
use those tools to do

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:01.230
more of an installment directly
on the machines themselves,

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:02.770
how does that look?

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:06.270
JERZY FORYCIARZ: So
they can do it-- first

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.010
of all they can do it directly.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:10.410
They can load the code directly,
the application directly

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:14.430
onto the machine using
just a startups grid

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:17.627
or baking an image, or
using Deployment Manager.

00:21:17.627 --> 00:21:19.960
These are the tools that are
provided with the platform.

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:20.460
Right?

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:23.850
But obviously we are integrated
with Chef, Puppet, TerraForm,

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:25.500
Deployment Manager.

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:30.090
And all of these tools have
plug-ins or connections

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:33.070
so that you can use them
to deploy instances that

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:34.890
would be part of the
Managed Instance Group

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.490
and then would use all of
the goodies off the platform.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:40.500
It's there.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.060
You can find all of
the documentation

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:48.855
on our documentation page, along
with examples and some case

00:21:48.855 --> 00:21:49.574
tests.

00:21:49.574 --> 00:21:50.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:54.330
We'll have links to all
of that documentation.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:57.510
Do you have a
little bit of a view

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:02.100
on what are the tools that
people are actually using most?

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:03.860
Do people use a
lot of TerraForm,

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:06.317
or have you seen people
using Puppet mostly?

00:22:06.317 --> 00:22:07.650
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ: I'll jump in.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.140
We've seen-- it's a fair mix.

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:12.780
I think the nice thing about
the Managed Instance Groups

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:15.730
is that, because they're
just built into GCE

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:19.530
itself and they're supported
by API, GCloud, and UI,

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:22.050
we have people using
Managed Instance Groups

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:23.370
from all of those entry points.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:26.970
So you can basically just--
when you're starting out,

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:29.940
you can just go into the UI and
create an instance group based

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.354
on a template or-- create
an image, create a template,

00:22:32.354 --> 00:22:33.270
create your own group.

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:34.920
And that's very
easy to get started

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:36.930
with because you can
see it, feel it, get

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:40.170
your hands dirty with
this very intuitive view

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:42.520
of the group of instances.

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:45.154
And then you can graduate,
if you want to, using GCloud

00:22:45.154 --> 00:22:47.070
if you want to be doing
some simple scripting.

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:49.620
And then, of course, if you're
building something more solid,

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.570
you go to the API.

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:54.090
But then what we are seeing,
of course with Chef, Puppet,

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:56.760
is that people are creating
their own cookbooks

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.200
and scripts.

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.560
And we're looking at building
some of those on our own

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:04.670
as well so that we can really
support the Managed Instance

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:10.316
Groups as a first class citizen
of these different cookbooks.

00:23:10.316 --> 00:23:12.690
So you can just create a group
and have it may be managed

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:13.800
externally, .

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:16.410
And then of course with
even our internal deployment

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.570
stack-- so like the
Deployment Manager--

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.000
you can use that to create
Managed Instance Groups

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.660
and that's another
common use case.

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:27.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah that
is Mark's favorite product

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:29.299
of all Google Cloud platform.

00:23:29.299 --> 00:23:30.340
He always talks about it.

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:32.490
MARK MANDEL: I do talk
about Deployment Manager.

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:32.950
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ: Nice.

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:33.450
Nice.

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:34.560
Well we do like it.

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.184
MARK MANDEL: I just want people
to script their deploys, that's

00:23:37.184 --> 00:23:38.090
all I want.

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:39.840
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ:
Infrastructure is code.

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:41.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
there's something

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:45.510
that I think is really
cool, and most people

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:49.020
tend to forget, which is you
can actually create and manage

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:51.557
Instance Groups with
preemptible machines, right?

00:23:51.557 --> 00:23:52.890
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ: That's right.

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:54.661
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
could you give us

00:23:54.661 --> 00:23:56.910
a little bit about-- could
you talk to us a little bit

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:59.040
about what are the
benefits of these?

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:03.300
And real quick, why
people would use them?

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:06.180
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ: I mean the
why is just to save money.

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:10.140
I mean that's-- the preemptibles
are there if you want to save

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:10.740
on cost.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:13.110
But at the same time, if
you're willing to give up

00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:15.000
the control of the instances.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.640
And the nice thing is that
if you use Managed Instance

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:19.980
Groups you already
have this mindset of,

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:22.580
each instance is replaceable.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:23.490
It's stateless.

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:26.940
I can create, destroy them,
because they'll just connect,

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:29.580
get their work, and do
it and then upload it

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:31.570
or do whatever needs to be done.

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:33.646
But each specific
instance isn't special.

00:24:33.646 --> 00:24:35.020
And so if you
accept that mindset

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:37.640
and now you're writing a Managed
Instance Group with 2,000

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:41.230
instances, and any one of them
can be replaced at any time,

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:42.880
then suddenly
preemptibles makes sense.

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:46.120
Because maybe preempt a
bunch of them-- that's OK.

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.690
Because auto scaling or
whatever other automation

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:50.870
you have running will
just create new ones

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:54.140
and you can keep going
and keep working,

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:56.230
but you keep your savings.

00:24:56.230 --> 00:24:57.110
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:59.680
I just, for fun, I want to ask.

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:02.184
So what are the coolest
things you've seen

00:25:02.184 --> 00:25:03.850
built on top of Managed
Infrastructures?

00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:05.770
Has there been a
particularly awesome project

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:08.010
that you thought was
particularly fascinating?

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:12.050
JERZY FORYCIARZ: So we've seen
a couple of interesting events,

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:15.550
one of which was one of our
colleagues decided-- when

00:25:15.550 --> 00:25:20.585
we first rolled out a beta
version of Auto Scaler,

00:25:20.585 --> 00:25:23.380
one of our decided
we can actually

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:27.080
show on stage that we
can run back to serving

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:29.040
1 million words per second.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:29.740
Right?

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:35.620
So we did an onstage demo,
pulling a load generator

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:39.730
and starting it, and then
creating from scratch a Managed

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:45.340
Instance Group running off
the shelf Abaci and loading it

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:48.190
with the load so that it
started adding instances.

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:52.160
And within six minutes,
it was adding--

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.650
the load was growing
gradually, right?

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:58.420
And within six minutes
we were already

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:00.890
supporting 1 million
words per second.

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:03.120
So can you beat that?

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:07.060
But one other
interesting story is

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:14.290
one of our customers, who was
migrating partly from on-prem

00:26:14.290 --> 00:26:17.740
and partly from a different
cloud provider, was

00:26:17.740 --> 00:26:21.220
having these spiky loads.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:23.240
And this was an
application back end.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:26.140
So it was super important
for this customer

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:29.970
not to lose these requests.

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:33.770
And this customer
came and said, look,

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:37.120
I really need a predictive
this auto scale.

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:38.860
And predictive auto
scaling is something

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:43.300
where you will be
able to say, tomorrow

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:46.240
at 7:00 AM I'm
going to have to use

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:48.010
this many instances of this.

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:50.630
And I'll automatically adjust.

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:54.220
And we didn't have
it at the time.

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:55.420
And they told us a story.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:58.540
And the story was, well,
your competition also

00:26:58.540 --> 00:26:59.230
didn't have it.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:02.880
So we ended up
disconnecting auto scaler

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.840
around the time when
we most needed it.

00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:08.530
And then we ended up adding
these instances manually

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:12.190
based on some back of
the envelope calculations

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:13.660
that we tracked
in a spreadsheet.

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:14.160
Right?

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:15.950
And it was a
terrible experience.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:18.870
So I'll need like 10 minutes
after the peak has happened.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:22.000
We were reconnecting
the auto scaler

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:25.340
so that it would do the clean
up and mostly scale down.

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:26.110
All that, right?

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:28.730
So they were miserable.

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:31.190
So we said, we'll be
happy to work with you.

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.220
But can you just
try our auto scaler?

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:34.140
And they said, no.

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.290
Look, you don't understand.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:41.140
We've used the auto scaler
on your competitor's cloud

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:42.040
for years.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:44.410
It's never going to
work in this condition.

00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:45.240
And we said, OK.

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:47.871
We'll promise you we're going
to build it, but just try it.

00:27:47.871 --> 00:27:48.370
Right?

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:51.055
And a couple of months
later, we got an email.

00:27:51.055 --> 00:27:52.555
And the email was
just one sentence.

00:27:52.555 --> 00:27:56.980
It said, "Predictive auto
scaling is no longer necessary.

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:58.110
Thank you."

00:27:58.110 --> 00:27:58.860
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:27:59.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:01.480
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ:
Yeah, for mine

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:04.690
I think the coolest thing I see
is some of the things people

00:28:04.690 --> 00:28:07.360
build with the
custom metric, right?

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:09.370
So auto scaling
everyone understands

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:14.845
to be CPU bound or based, or QPS
based-- which is like traffic.

00:28:14.845 --> 00:28:16.720
But you can actually
code up your own metric,

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:20.320
which could be anything you
want at all, and scale on that.

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:23.260
And that's what some
of the customers

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:26.620
have been using to build
different cube-based scaling

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:28.450
solutions that are a
little bit smarter.

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:32.750
So that as the work comes in,
the metric gets calculated.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:35.320
And then the auto scaler just
listens to that and then scales

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:37.210
up and down to
process the work you

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:40.420
have based on your own
definition of what work means

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:44.637
and how much time you need per
work unit and per instance.

00:28:44.637 --> 00:28:46.720
And I think there's a lot
of flexibility with that

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:48.892
because you have that
full control of how

00:28:48.892 --> 00:28:49.600
you calculate it.

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:52.280
And then we'll just scale it
based on how you want it scaled

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:54.160
and get through your work.

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:56.804
And then scale you down when
you don't need the machines.

00:28:56.804 --> 00:28:57.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

