00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:25.590
So the question is, OK, imagine
that I have a Kubernetes

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:26.180
cluster.

00:32:26.180 --> 00:32:27.890
And I have a service
that I expose.

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:29.670
And I expose it to the world.

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:33.430
But I want to make sure
that only some specific IP's

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:37.180
or maybe a range of IP's
is able to access it.

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:39.330
How do I do that?

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:41.510
MARK MANDEL: So it's
actually surprisingly easy,

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:43.870
which is really, really cool.

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:47.660
You're able to restrict access
to a load balancer service

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:50.330
to an IP address range
in your YAML file,

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:53.060
in the same way as when
you define your service.

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.490
There is an argument in
there that you can set

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:57.680
called Load Balancer
Source Ranges.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:32:59.930
It's a C-I-D-R. "Cedar"?

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:01.600
I don't know how
you pronounce that.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:02.500
C-I-D-R?

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:03.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
pronounce it "cider."

00:33:03.710 --> 00:33:04.160
MARK MANDEL: Cider?

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:04.540
CIDR.

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:04.780
OK.

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:05.740
I can work with that.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:08.200
It's one of those things
you read on the internet

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:10.370
but you never hear
someone say out loud.

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:12.770
So it's an IP range
format, for those

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:14.050
who aren't familiar with it.

00:33:14.050 --> 00:33:16.580
So you can specify
what IP range you want.

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:19.850
So if you've got a specific
set, maybe a particularly white

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:22.700
listed IP or a particularly
white listed set of IP's.

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:23.660
Very simple to do that.

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:26.030
It ends up translating
behind the scenes

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:29.990
to being firewall specifications
inside Google Cloud.

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:33.380
And then only those IP addresses
can connect their services.

00:33:33.380 --> 00:33:36.620
So you have better security if
you want only particular things

00:33:36.620 --> 00:33:39.140
to access the services you
have available on Kubernetes.

00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:40.056
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:33:40.056 --> 00:33:43.310
I think that the important thing
is that, the same way we're

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:45.750
saying that you can do this
on Kubernetes very easily,

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:49.290
this ends up being translated
as just one more firewall rule

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:52.450
that will be applied to
the load balancer generated

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:54.290
on Google Compute Engine.

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:57.214
So if you want to do that by
yourself, you can also do that.

00:33:57.214 --> 00:33:58.880
If you're not running
Kubernetes and you

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:03.890
want to limit the-- to constrain
the range of IP's that you're

00:34:03.890 --> 00:34:07.490
exposing your service to, you
can use GCloud Compute Firewall

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:09.790
rules and do exactly the same.

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:10.540
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:11.039
Yeah.

00:34:11.039 --> 00:34:13.530
So it's all the same
things under the hood.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:15.920
And if you're not
using Kubernetes on us,

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:19.889
maybe you're doing it on AWS,
it's supported there as well.

00:34:19.889 --> 00:34:21.704
So that's pretty cool as well.

00:34:21.704 --> 00:34:22.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:26.900
And the last thing is, if you
do this and for any reason

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:29.120
you're running Kubernetes
on a provider that

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:32.940
doesn't support this feature,
it will just be ignored.

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:34.550
So be careful with
that, I guess.

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:35.300
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:36.000
Excellent.

